  "findings": [
    "All 32 synthetic-run security reports pass with full protected-record completeness, unique ingress IDs and reconstructability despite physical output inversions.",
    "Only 15/32 trials pass strict normal functional acceptance (no fail-closed and no deadline breaches).",
    "8 platform: 3/4; 8 virtual: 2/4; 10 platform: 4/4; 10 virtual: 2/4; 11 each: 1/4; 12 each: 1/4. The pass probability is not monotonically determined by caller count in the observed trials.",
    "311 fail-closed outputs were counted separately from successful masking, all due to deadlines; zero bounded-queue overload rejections across all configurations. Queue-capacity expansion alone will not address observed failures.",
    "Round 3 showed correlated severe deadline degradation at eight callers: platform 74/200 fail-closed, virtual 52/200. A subsequent eight-platform run passed with lower tail latency. This supports investigation of transient host/ONNX execution variability, not merely a fixed caller count threshold.",
    "Median caller p95 alone hides failures: at 11 platform median p95=1518 ms despite functional passes only 1/4; always inspect p99, individual exceedances, fail-closed counts and max.",
    "A 4/4 pass at 10 platform callers is not robust capacity proof because 8 platform runs failed in this study and earlier one-shot 10-platform run also failed.",
    "Native ONNX workload is not equivalent in failed trials because timed-out requests abort before normal completion; compare successful workload totals, not just overall protected throughput.",
    "Endpoint working-set samples ~698-714 MiB after work and ~167-185 MiB after shutdown do not measure in-flight peaks, native leak rate or production stability."
  ],
  "recommended_next_step": {
    "target": "Closed-loop burst benchmarks should transition to reproducible steady-arrival/open-loop load and a longer soak with peak RSS sampling, per-request stage service timing, real worker queue wait, host CPU frequency/load and JVM GC/JFR metrics.",
    "experiment": "Start fixed-rate logging at approximately 5-7 normally masked events/s, with one or more steady-rate producers, then increase offered rate in measured increments while keeping ONNX worker, 2s deadline, queue and model fixed. Distinguish offered/admitted/processed events and deadline/overload outcomes.",
    "gate": "Every record must have a protected unique ingress identity; fail-closed output must never contain raw fixtures. Accept only sustained zero fail-closed for the supported rate together with p99/max and memory stability across repeated longer observation windows.",
    "caveat": "No guarantee of overall workload security, absolute safe concurrency count or production SLA follows from two-to-four repetitions of synthetic burst tests."
  }
}
